      "claim": "Alfuzosin, which shares diaminopyrimidine/quinazoline, dimethoxyaryl, and amide motifs with the query, has absolute oral bioavailability of 49% under fed conditions, supporting F >= 20% for this scaffold class.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C04", "C24", "C25"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Colchicine, a methoxy-rich tricyclic amide with P-gp-limited absorption, has mean absolute oral bioavailability around 37-55% across formulations and populations, indicating that high lipophilicity and polar surface area do not preclude F >= 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C02", "C10", "C16", "C17", "C18", "C20", "C21", "C23", "C05", "C06", "C19"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Indomethacin, an indole/chlorophenyl/methoxy carboxylic acid, has absolute bioavailability of 76-80% in sustained-release form, supporting oral absorption of indole-chlorophenyl-containing structures.",
      "card_ids": ["C07", "C14"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Rigosertib, a methoxy-rich molecule, has absolute oral bioavailability of 34.8-35% in fasted patients, and GDC-0810, a chlorophenyl indole-like molecule, was predicted at about 45%, both above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C08", "C11", "C13"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Additional high-F records for omeprazole, loratadine, midodrine, and 5-HTP provide further context that diverse oral drugs exceed 20% despite varying physicochemical properties.",
      "card_ids": ["C03", "C09", "C12", "C15", "C22", "C26", "C27"]
    }
  ],
